# Rejected: arXiv:1610.03115

This paper does not yield an acceptable native problem family under the stated
gates.  The attempted necklace family passes **G** and **V**, but fails **H** on
both tracks and fails G9(c).  The retained implementation is
`rejected_gen_1610_03115.py`; its corrected `selftest_report.json` records the
failure rather than claiming that the family ships.

## Paper facts used at Step 0

Section 1 of Benson et al., [*Note on Nordhaus-Gaddum problems for power
domination*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.03115), defines the executable two-stage
power-domination process.  The paragraph before Theorem 2.17 defines the
necklace graph `N_r` from `r` copies of `K4-e`; Theorem 2.17 states the cubic
upper bound and that necklaces attain it; Remark 2.16 supplies the local
obstruction used to certify that every minimum witness meets every diamond.
Theorem 3.13 extends the cubic bound componentwise.  These results license the
retained composition generator and make exact verification straightforward.

The same paper also identifies the easy constructions.  Lemma 2.18 gives an
explicit two-vertex solution for the complement of a necklace.  Theorem 2.12
constrains the extremal `n/3` case to visibly foliated components (plus
`K3,3`), and Lemma 2.13 gives a one-vertex solution in the relevant complement.
The tight examples `rK3`, paths, combs, and the order-8/order-11 graphs are
likewise explicit or fixed-size.  No theorem in the paper establishes
algorithmic or distributional hardness.

## Gate decision

| gate | result |
|---|---|
| G | passes for the attempted family: sample one vertex in each necklace diamond before relabelling; no generated instance is solved during construction |
| V | passes: the checker validates shape, simulates domination and forcing exactly, and accepts any valid size-`r` set |
| H, Track A | fails: arbitrary relabelling does not hide the true-twin signature distribution from an efficient construction-aware scan, and the paper supplies no hard parameter regime for this distribution |
| H, Track B | fails: the alleged compact route is the same full closed-neighborhood signature scan as the mechanical algorithm, so there is no compression gap |
| G9(c) | fails at the attempted shipping preset: 15,936 exact signature operations exceed the 300-operation cap |

## Mechanical cost versus compact route

At the attempted 249-diamond preset the graph has 996 vertices and 1,494
edges.  Sorting/hashing each four-element closed neighborhood recovers every
true-twin pair and returns a verified answer on 8/8 seeds.  The retained
measurement is 15,936 counted primitive signature operations and 0.00166 s
mean wall-clock time (0.00363 s maximum in the latest run).

There is no shorter executable route after noticing the paper's decomposition.
Because labels are permuted arbitrarily, the solver must still locate the
diamonds by constructing and grouping those same closed-neighborhood
signatures.  Thus the compact route is also 15,936 counted operations, not the
previously claimed 261.  Saying “choose one vertex per diamond” suppresses the
entire cost of finding the diamonds and is not an executable route on the
rendered instance.

Shrinking does not open a valid window.  Under the declared structured prior,
the exact guess density is `4^r / C(4r,r)`; it first drops below `1e-6` at
`r=19`.  The identical signature route already costs `64r = 1,216` counted
operations there.  Conversely, the largest size within the 300-operation cap
is `r=4` (256 operations), where the exact valid-answer density is about
0.14066.  Therefore no size in this construction can pass both G4 and G9(c).

This is not a `cap_bound` case: the answer at `r=19` has only 19 atoms, while
the binding failure is that the only reliable route is a linear scan rather
than a compact insight.  The failed OpenRouter calls retained in the transcript
were HTTP 403 errors and are not counted as hardness evidence or as a reason
for rejection.
